[{:db/id "q-19039/selection/0cc935bc9a2e79ae47f860944aca606a75c3302c4048bf9d4a71dc9599b86087" :answer/id #uuid "b836eab1-38bc-5124-b36f-5fb44822a862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{41.76}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19039/selection/2b70365b330e910b66988567c99a87adc47bd52821686406cba25dc409452013" :answer/id #uuid "c061d394-d848-5dee-9316-94f6fcfbf672" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{88.21}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19039/selection/9aaba355c86df4e360269dfbc7d8c2550183a35bdc089c4626e9d9e556a6afba" :answer/id #uuid "9b4902ce-263f-5b15-8184-f513b8aff691" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{54.98}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19039/selection/ee854069c7966600f8177e4b377d9b9abc84fab6acfbad0fb03eb514eb8f8770" :answer/id #uuid "514824aa-9790-5c13-a304-5c5442145366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{63.23}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19039/selection/f13e74f74d2a1937ea5d5b9ee856ecfd452c9b22994fc2e3fa12dcc660cd9508" :answer/id #uuid "bbbaef10-ea83-5887-9717-1fa8fe0c94f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60.87}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19039/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0d1364-0fc3-564e-89e6-a0ee33d1755c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-19039/selection/ee854069c7966600f8177e4b377d9b9abc84fab6acfbad0fb03eb514eb8f8770" :answer-field/choices ["q-19039/selection/0cc935bc9a2e79ae47f860944aca606a75c3302c4048bf9d4a71dc9599b86087" "q-19039/selection/2b70365b330e910b66988567c99a87adc47bd52821686406cba25dc409452013" "q-19039/selection/9aaba355c86df4e360269dfbc7d8c2550183a35bdc089c4626e9d9e556a6afba" "q-19039/selection/ee854069c7966600f8177e4b377d9b9abc84fab6acfbad0fb03eb514eb8f8770" "q-19039/selection/f13e74f74d2a1937ea5d5b9ee856ecfd452c9b22994fc2e3fa12dcc660cd9508"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19039" :question/id #uuid "2b94aa63-fdb6-5ed2-a8a2-4a1a717613d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-19039" :question/problem "Given that $m\\cdot n=10$, find the angle between $m=⟨t,-t,3⟩$ and $n=⟨3,2,4⟩.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, since $m\\cdot n=10$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\cdot n & =10 \\\\ t\\cdot 3+(-t)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 & =10 \\\\ 3t-2t+12 & =10 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =⟨(-2),-(-2),3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $m$ and $n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{m\\cdot n}{|\\,m\\,||\\,n\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{m}_{x}^{2}+{m}_{y}^{2}+{m}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{n}_{x}^{2}+{n}_{y}^{2}+{n}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{493}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{493}})≈{63.23}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-19039/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-19039"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-69831"] :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,1,2⟩,\\,q=⟨1,3,2⟩,\\,r=⟨4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071968/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨4,5,-2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})≈{63}^{∘}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-69833"] :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,y=⟨-2,2,3⟩,\\,z=⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071968/assets/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-77159"] :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,q=⟨3,4,2⟩,\\,r=⟨3,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071968/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨3,3,1⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
